# Railway builds from a git clone, so it only ever sees tracked files. A local
# `docker build` sees the whole working directory instead — including the
# virtualenv, which is 940 MB of macOS arm64 binaries that a linux/amd64 image
# cannot execute and does not need, since the image installs its own.
#
# Without this file the two builds produce different images from the same
# commit, and the local one is the misleading one.

# virtualenvs
.venv/
venv/
backend/.venv/
env/

# python bytecode. Same ** rule as above — without it, backend/tests/__pycache__
# ships, full of .pyc files compiled by the host's Python 3.12 for an image that
# runs 3.11. They are ignored at runtime, just dead weight and confusion.
**/__pycache__/
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info/
**/.pytest_cache/

# tests and the eval harness. Neither is reachable from a served request, and
# the results JSON is read by humans in the repo, not by the app.
backend/tests/
backend/evals/

# secrets — the image gets its key from the environment at runtime.
#
# The ** matters. Docker matches these patterns against the path relative to
# the context root, so a bare `.env` excludes only ./.env and would happily
# copy backend/.env — the one that actually holds the key — into the image.
# Verified by grepping the built image for the key prefix; see the commit.
**/.env
**/*.env
!**/.env.example

# derived eval data. Judge verdicts are re-fetchable and the results files are
# read by humans, not by the app.
backend/evals/.cache/

# vcs and editor noise
.git/
.gitignore
.github/
.vscode/
.idea/
.DS_Store

# docs and planning — none of it is served
docs/
tasks/
notes.txt
*.md
RUN.txt

# local tooling
.claude/
docker-compose.yml
